WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:07.400
The first film showed one nuclear receptor binding both ligand and DNA.

2
00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:13.040
How does a tiny molecule inside the pocket flip a gene from off to on?

3
00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:19.100
And why can one drug block estrogen in breast tissue yet imitate it in the uterus?

4
00:00:20.233 --> 00:00:25.453
Zoom into the hormone-binding domain, built from about 12 helices around a

5
00:00:25.453 --> 00:00:26.513
hydrophobic pocket.

6
00:00:27.813 --> 00:00:31.113
Its last helix, helix 12, is the main moving part.

7
00:00:32.413 --> 00:00:36.553
When an agonist like estradiol enters, it switches the receptor on.

8
00:00:37.813 --> 00:00:41.473
Helix 12 folds shut across the mouth of the pocket like a lid.

9
00:00:42.517 --> 00:00:47.687
Closed like that, helix 12 completes a shallow surface groove called AF-2.

10
00:00:47.687 --> 00:00:51.617
Co-activators carry a short helix that fits it.

11
00:00:52.937 --> 00:00:58.717
Docked co-activators bring enzymes that add acetyl tags to histones, the spools DNA

12
00:00:58.717 --> 00:01:04.997
wraps around. The DNA loosens, and RNA polymerase starts copying the gene.

13
00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:10.560
An antagonist fills the same pocket without switching the receptor on.

14
00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:17.280
The liver converts the breast-cancer drug tamoxifen into tighter-binding forms like

15
00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:18.920
4-hydroxytamoxifen.

16
00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:24.060
It fits the pocket, but one long side chain sticks out of the opening.

17
00:01:25.133 --> 00:01:29.713
That protruding side chain sits right where helix 12 wants to fold shut.

18
00:01:31.153 --> 00:01:33.753
Notice the nearby notch where co-activators dock.

19
00:01:35.033 --> 00:01:38.233
If helix 12 cannot close, what happens to that groove?

20
00:01:44.217 --> 00:01:49.497
The receptor's own helix takes the groove, because helix 12 cannot close as a lid.

21
00:01:50.817 --> 00:01:55.077
Instead it swings around and lies inside the co-activator groove itself.

22
00:01:56.337 --> 00:01:57.897
That turns the lid into a plug.

23
00:01:59.097 --> 00:02:04.997
With the AF-2 groove occupied, co-activators cannot bind there, so breast cells keep

24
00:02:04.997 --> 00:02:05.997
the gene off.

25
00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:12.060
Yet tamoxifen still lets the receptor pair on DNA, leaving a second switching

26
00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:18.480
surface exposed. That front-end surface, AF-1, does not depend on helix 12.

27
00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:23.360
So the outcome can depend on the helper proteins each tissue makes.

28
00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:29.620
Cells rich in co-activators that work through AF-1 can read the same drug as on.

29
00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:36.510
That helps explain why tamoxifen opposes estrogen in breast tissue yet imitates it

30
00:02:36.510 --> 00:02:37.690
in uterus and bone.

31
00:02:39.110 --> 00:02:44.930
Either way, the receptor works through new RNA and protein, unlike fast cyclic AMP

32
00:02:44.930 --> 00:02:51.730
relays. The first new messenger RNA appears within tens of minutes, and protein

33
00:02:51.730 --> 00:02:53.310
changes build over hours.

34
00:02:54.500 --> 00:03:00.380
In return, those new proteins remain after the hormone clears, and one receptor can

35
00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:01.820
reset many genes together.

36
00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:05.540
A drug is not simply on or off by itself.

37
00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:10.600
Ligand shape and each cell's helpers decide whether genes turn on.
